+</Section>
+
+<Section><Heading>Optional Voronoi backends</Heading>
+An external package can register a provider using
+<C>RegisterVoronoiBackend(name, provider)</C>, where <C>provider</C> is a
+function of one argument. The name must be a nonempty string other than
+<C>"magma"</C>. Registering an existing name replaces its provider.
+<P/>
+The general entry point is <C>VoronoiComplex(request : backend := name)</C>.
+It requires a record and an explicitly selected registered provider. HAP
+passes the record unchanged; the provider interprets and validates its
+contents. The interface imposes no particular field degree, rank or group
+family. For example, a provider may accept the following description:
+<Log><![CDATA[
+request := rec(
+    group := "GL", rank := 2,
+    field := rec(kind := "quadratic", discriminant := -7),
+    order := rec(kind := "maximal"),
+    lattice := rec(kind := "free"));
+K := VoronoiComplex(request : backend := "julia");
+]]></Log>
+Field polynomials, order bases, lattice ideals or subgroup data can be
+included when a provider supports them. This does not assert that any
+particular provider implements those cases. A provider returns a HAP
+non-free resolution or <C>fail</C> for an unsupported request; it may also
+raise an error explaining the limitation. Failure never falls back to Magma.
+<P/>
+The existing functions <C>VoronoiComplexGL(n,d)</C> and
+<C>VoronoiComplexSL(n,d)</C> also accept the option <C>backend</C>.
+When selecting a registered provider, these construct a record with
+<C>group</C> equal to <C>"GL"</C> or <C>"SL"</C>, <C>rank := n</C>,
+<C>field := rec(kind := "quadratic", radicand := d)</C>,
+<C>order := rec(kind := "maximal")</C> and
+<C>lattice := rec(kind := "free")</C>. Here <C>d</C> is a squarefree
+radicand, not a field discriminant. Without the backend option, or with
+<C>backend := "magma"</C>, these two convenience functions retain the
+Magma implementation described above.
+<P/>
+A Julia package running with GAP.jl can register an in-process callback
+under the name <C>"julia"</C>. HAP itself does not install or load Julia.
+An unregistered backend name is an error. Providers must supply complete stabilizers, the orientation action
+and equivariant boundaries; perfect forms or a group presentation alone
+do not suffice for higher homology. Standard HAP operations such as
+<C>FreeGResolution</C> and <C>TensorWithIntegers</C> then apply to the
+returned object. A contracting homotopy is a separate capability and must
+not be inferred merely from the existence of a resolution.
 </Section>
 </Chapter>
